---

*On How Media Shapes Architecture*

For most of architecture’s history, the building came first and the image followed. A building was designed, constructed, occupied, and then photographed. Increasingly, that sequence can work in reverse. The anticipated photograph begins to shape the room that will eventually produce it.

The Museum of Ice Cream makes this reversal easy to see.

Its rooms are organized around simple, recognizable encounters: a pool filled with oversized sprinkles, a pink subway car, enormous pieces of candy, and monochromatic interiors. Each environment is easy to understand and photograph almost immediately. Visitors don’t need to understand the building’s organization to know what to do inside. Stand here. Hold the pop-art prop. Take the picture for your grid. The sprinkle pool is therefore both a room and an image-making device. Its exaggerated color, recognizable objects, and contained visual field allow the architecture to survive the compression of a photograph. The visitor completes the space by entering the frame. This does not make the Museum of Ice Cream less architectural. It means photography has become part of its architectural program.

The company describes its spaces as interactive environments rather than conventional museums, and their success depends on physical participation. But that participation also generates an enormous secondary audience that never enters the building. The room is experienced by one group and circulated by another.

The economics suggest how consequential that circulation can become. Parent company Figure8 raised $40 million at a reported $200 million valuation while expanding the concept to multiple cities. What appears to be an unusual collection of rooms is also a repeatable spatial system: distinctive enough to attract attention and legible enough to reproduce across locations and across thousands of individual photographs.

The post is not simply documentation of the room. It is one of the things the room produces.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/molly-m-5.jpg" alt="Collage of old Windows desktop windows over a sky and grass background, framing the Museum of Ice Cream's sprinkle pool, a red interior, a crowd at a Montez Press Radio broadcast, event flyers and two DJs performing" pie="Collage on media virality" /%}

Montez Press Radio operates almost in reverse.

Its space is small and visually ordinary by comparison. Chairs, production equipment, and people: very little about the room announces itself as an architectural destination. Its importance instead comes from what happens there. Writers, artists, musicians, and other guests enter to create ever-evolving art, witness, and simply spend time together.

Here, the room does not need to circulate because the broadcast does.

This changes what architecture is being asked to provide. Montez does not need a succession of visually distinct environments; the same room can accommodate one program after another because its identity is continually reconstructed through its occupation. Its effective footprint is less a collection of specialized rooms than a schedule. At different hours, the same few square feet contain entirely different audiences, conversations, and communities.

Yet Montez also complicates any clean distinction between a photogenic institution and one unconcerned with images. Being present in the studio carries its own cultural visibility. Photographs of crowded broadcasts (performers, friends, equipment, and bodies gathered in a small room) communicate something the broadcast alone cannot: who was there. The architecture may not function as spectacle, but presence itself becomes an image.

Meow Wolf occupies a position between these two conditions.

Its installations are intensely visual but far harder to capture in a single photograph. Visitors to Omega Mart in Las Vegas first see something familiar: a supermarket. Products line shelves beneath fluorescents. Signs hawk absurd food products and make-believe brands. But doors, fridges, and openings lead away from the store into a spreading network of rooms.

The recognizable image becomes a door, not a destination. Once within, it’s difficult to get a clear picture of the project from any single vantage point. Spaces lead to other spaces. Narrative snippets appear in objects, videos, products, and hidden rooms. Visitors can photograph nearly everything, but a single photo rarely captures the whole story. The architecture produces images and refuses the spatial experience these images are meant to represent.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/molly-m-4.jpg" alt="Collage of a glowing purple and orange Meow Wolf installation set inside an old black-and-white photograph of a city block, as if the building had been opened up" pie="Collage made with photographs of Meow Wolf exhibits" /%}

This creates a different relation to circulation. The Museum of Ice Cream squeezes a room into a highly legible picture. Meow Wolf allows pieces of a far bigger universe to bleed into images without explaining how anything connects. The incomplete image is helpful. Someone who stumbled across it online might understand enough to be interested without understanding enough to feel they had already been there.

These three institutions reveal different ways architecture can operate within an image economy. The Museum of Ice Cream constructs spaces that become unusually complete photographs. Montez Press Radio constructs an ordinary room whose significance comes from the activity occupying it. Meow Wolf produces environments that generate extraordinary photographs while resisting complete explanation through them.

The most significant difference is between what the institutions broadcast and what their visitors broadcast. An institution may photograph its programming while its audience photographs the architecture. Or it may build an architectural image that visitors will mainly use as a backdrop for images of themselves. There is no longer one single authoritative photograph of the building. Its public image is created by the participation of thousands of visitors.

As Beatriz Colomina has argued, architecture and media were never separate; photographs, magazines, exhibitions, and television make buildings public. What has changed is the velocity and distribution of that process. Every visitor today carries a camera, publisher, and distribution network in his/her pocket. As a result, platforms reward what can be recognized quickly, categorized easily, and circulated repeatedly, and a complicated spatial experience eventually has to pass through a very small rectangle on a screen (Kyle Chayka on algorithmic culture).

But these examples do not indicate any single architectural response to that condition. A room can be more readable. It can be visually ordinary and let its activity circulate. Or it can generate a whole bunch of images, and none of them can explain the whole.

The question isn’t whether architecture has become “instagrammable.” Instagram has been around for a while, but images have mediated architecture far longer. The difference is that the image can now influence what is built, how it is occupied, and how far beyond its physical footprint an institution can function. The photo still follows the room. The room is increasingly waiting for it, however.
